## Context
Nomination from the sen-causal-reading session (2026-10-15). The author does
not want harness skills to overload runtime names and prefers draconic names
for user-facing skills.

Observed in Claude Code 2.1.300 (binary strings): `dream` is a native
background task type (auto-dream memory consolidation, setting
`autoDreamEnabled`, task label "dream"). No `/dream` slash command string was
found, so the overload is a label and concept collision with the native
consolidation, not a command shadowing. Cause of user confusion: two
different consolidations, two different stores, one name.

Candidate: `hoard` ("dragons in their lair, dreaming over the hoard").
Caveat: `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design-nomenclature.md` reserves
`hoard` for the memory library and its CLI verbs (`hoard assay`, `reckon`,
`seal`, `sift`, `shed`, `disown`, `weigh`), and states "the pass is /dream".
No code implements that library (no `hoard` file or package; memory v8
docs do not use the word). The author decides whether the reservation
stands; if it does, pick another draconic verb.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Author decision recorded: `hoard`, or another name, and the fate of
      the nomenclature reservation.
- [ ] Skill directory, frontmatter `name`, every reference (16 files in
      skills, rules, tests, Makefile, scripts, hooks, adapters, profiles;
      `lair` step 11, brood, roar) renamed in one commit; `memory/DREAM.md`
      and `memory/dreams/` paths decided explicitly (rename or keep).
- [ ] A transition alias `/dream` points to the new skill for one release,
      then is removed by a dated follow-up.
- [ ] `make skills-catalog` regenerated; `make check-skills-drift` and
      `make check` pass.
